### The harness assembles `src/scene.js` and calls YOUR module like this — nothing else runs
```js
const ctx = { THREE, scene, renderer, loaders, heightAt, env, assets };
const g = await build(ctx);            // the export MUST be named `build`, one argument
scene.add(g);                          // g must be a THREE.Group / Object3D
if (g.userData.update) g.userData.update(t, dt);   // the ONLY animation hook that runs
```
So `{{ zone_file }}` exports `export function build(ctx) → THREE.Group` named `"{{ zone_name }}"`.
`build{{ zone_name }}` and `userData.tick` are never called: if an asset carries its own
`userData.update`, cache it at build time and call it from the zone's `userData.update`.
`ctx` also gives you `ctx.env` (whatever `buildEnv` returned) and `ctx.heightAt`.

## What this zone is graded on
The recipes for all five points below are printed in full at the end of this brief
(**Recipes**) — copy their numbers and snippets; do not invent your own.

1. **Hit the density counts in the description.**  A zone with 5 objects in it reads as a token
   gesture; the counts are the difference between 0.5 and 0.8.  Props go **against** things
   (path edges, wall bases, under benches, in corners), never in a grid or a perfect ring, with
   >= 20 % positional jitter and full yaw jitter.
2. **Variation, not clones.**  Any repeated natural element needs >= 3 distinct silhouettes
   (different geometry, not just scale), per-instance scale jitter +/-30 %, hue jitter +/-0.03
   via `setColorAt`, full yaw jitter and a few degrees of lean.  Clustered placement (cluster
   seeds + members around them), never uniform scatter and never a ring of identical cones.
3. **Blend into the ground.**  Anything that meets the terrain sinks 3-5 cm into it and gets
   6-15 dressing pieces along the seam (tufts, pebbles, leaves).  A raised bed gets a chamfered
   kerb, not a naked box on grass.  NEVER lay a hard-edged disc or square (a pond, a gravel
   bed, a terrace, a lawn patch) flat on top of the ground: use the depression `heightAt`
   already carves for it and ring the seam with a kerb + dressing, or its outline shows from
   every overview camera (`flat_ground` / `floating_or_sunken_asset`).  Add the zone's share of
   ground cover (300-800 instanced tufts for a planted zone) unless the description says bare.
4. **Serve the cameras.**  Skip any SCATTER instance inside a ~4 m disc around an authored camera
   (write a zero-scale matrix, keep the index; `cameraMask` in the cookbook) — a tree 1 m in front
   of the establishing camera ruins the shot.  Then, by hand (not scatter, so the mask does not
   remove it), place ONE element 2.5-4 m from a camera that looks across this zone, off to one
   side, as its foreground frame.
5. **Motion that shows.**  Amplitudes from the Recipes table (foliage +/-0.10-0.20 rad, particles
   >= 0.6 m per 1.5 s).  The harness DIFFERENCES the t=0 and t=1.5 frames pixel by pixel and
   reports the number: too small a change counts as `nothing_moves` (-0.05 and the animation
   criterion).  Sub-centimetre sway is invisible at 1024x576.  Give this
   zone at least one motion big enough to see across the frame, and everything a pure function of `t`.
6. Use `InstancedMesh` for anything repeated > 5x.

